---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const internal = (segment) => `${base}/${segment.replace(/^\//, '')}`;

const lineage = [
  {
    title: 'Agalmics / Agalmic Economics',
    status: 'Established antecedent + contemporary extension',
    prior: 'Robert Levin’s late-1990s “Agalmics: The Marginalization of Scarcity” already develops agalmics around non-scarce goods and their relation to scarce inputs.',
    position: 'Agalmic Research uses the existing term for a contemporary programme centred on machine cognition, knowledge production and abundance-era institutions. It does not claim to originate agalmics.',
    sources: [['Robert Levin · Agalmics', 'https://rednsx.org/~dtinker/agalmics.html']],
  },
  {
    title: 'Displaced Scarcity Hypothesis',
    status: 'Established mechanisms + synthesis; formal novelty unassessed',
    prior: 'Simon’s attention scarcity, fixed-proportions bottlenecks, Teece’s complementary assets, Weitzman’s idea-processing constraint, strong-complementarity models and recent AI-abundance papers all anticipate substantial parts of the mechanism.',
    position: '“Law” is withdrawn. The useful research task is formal: identify when an abundance shock changes binding constraints, shadow values, rents or market power, and distinguish complementarity, substitutability and induced-demand regimes.',
    sources: [
      ['Simon 1971', 'https://gwern.net/doc/design/1971-simon.pdf'],
      ['Teece 1986', 'https://doi.org/10.1016/0048-7333(86)90027-2'],
      ['Weitzman 1998', 'https://doi.org/10.1162/003355398555595'],
      ['Wu 2026', 'https://papers.ssrn.com/sol3/papers.cfm?abstract_id=7183798'],
    ],
  },
  {
    title: 'Economics of Epistemic Abundance',
    status: 'Renamed synthesis / application',
    prior: 'Anna Grandori already published “Epistemic Economics and Organization”; Arrow and later knowledge-economics traditions address invention and information.',
    position: 'The programme uses “Economics of Epistemic Abundance” for its narrower question: allocation when candidate knowledge becomes cheap while verification, judgment, attention, trust and realization remain scarce.',
    sources: [
      ['Grandori 2013', 'https://www.routledge.com/Epistemic-Economics-and-Organization-Forms-of-Rationality-and-Governance/Grandori/p/book/9781138906112'],
      ['Arrow 1962', 'https://www.nber.org/books-and-chapters/rate-and-direction-inventive-activity-economic-and-social-factors/economic-welfare-and-allocation-resources-invention'],
    ],
  },
  {
    title: 'Innovation Search Under AI',
    status: 'Established search theory + AI-era extension',
    prior: 'Search is central to evolutionary economics; recombinant growth and adjacent-possible frameworks are established. Recent evidence also suggests AI can alter the direction of scientific search, not merely its speed.',
    position: 'The prospective contribution is the effect of machine cognition on search cost, branching, recombination, search direction, selection pressure and realization.',
    sources: [
      ['Taalbi · adjacent possible', 'https://doi.org/10.1093/icc/dtaf028'],
      ['Hao et al. 2026', 'https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-025-09922-y'],
    ],
  },
  {
    title: 'Authorial Capacity Constraint',
    status: 'Close prior art · synthesis/terminology only',
    prior: 'Fernandes et al., Maier, O’Keefe and van Zoonen et al. directly address gaps between AI-assisted artefact performance, human understanding, metacognitive calibration and claim defensibility.',
    position: 'The label is retained as an organizing construct while explicitly withdrawing any implication that Agalmic Research first discovered the underlying phenomenon.',
    sources: [
      ['Fernandes et al. 2026', 'https://doi.org/10.1016/j.chb.2025.108779'],
      ['Maier 2026', 'https://doi.org/10.3390/jintelligence14070112'],
      ['O’Keefe 2026', 'https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.7155879'],
      ['van Zoonen et al. 2026', 'https://doi.org/10.1016/j.emj.2026.06.001'],
    ],
  },
  {
    title: 'Defensible stewardship',
    status: 'Close antecedent · local operating synthesis',
    prior: 'Claim accountability already argues that a named human should be able to reconstruct and defend scholarly claims. BMJ contributorship and guarantorship predate the current AI debate.',
    position: '“Epistemic authority should not exceed defensibility” is an Agalmic Research operating maxim, not a claim of first discovery. The research problem is operationalizing authority evidence without creating theatre.',
    sources: [
      ['Beyond AI disclosure', 'https://doi.org/10.1016/j.emj.2026.06.001'],
      ['BMJ contributorship', 'https://doi.org/10.1136/bmj.315.7110.696'],
    ],
  },
  {
    title: 'Contribution roles and epistemic authority',
    status: 'Established contributorship + authority-layer extension',
    prior: 'CRediT standardizes fourteen contributor roles, and contributorship models have separated “who did what” from the byline for decades.',
    position: 'Use CRediT instead of inventing another contribution taxonomy. The experimental extension is a separate, claim-sensitive record of who or what can warrant which claims and why.',
    sources: [
      ['CRediT', 'https://credit.niso.org/'],
      ['ANSI/NISO Z39.104-2022', 'https://www.niso.org/publications/z39104-2022-credit'],
    ],
  },
  {
    title: 'Epistemic handoff and uptake',
    status: 'Established brokerage/uptake + narrow protocol extension',
    prior: 'Absorptive capacity, transfer stickiness, knowledge brokers, innovation intermediaries, boundary spanning, transactive memory and expert-jury bottlenecks all cover important parts of routing and uptake.',
    position: '“Epistemic handoff” is retained only for a narrow, claim-sensitive transfer: preserve origin and contribution, state the authority gap, route toward a specified missing epistemic function, and record what warrant the recipient actually adds.',
    sources: [
      ['Cohen & Levinthal 1990', 'https://doi.org/10.2307/2393553'],
      ['Howells 2006', 'https://doi.org/10.1016/j.respol.2006.03.005'],
      ['Meyer 2010', 'https://doi.org/10.1177/1075547009359797'],
      ['Gimpel et al. 2025', 'https://doi.org/10.1007/s10726-025-09935-y'],
    ],
  },
  {
    title: 'Distributed discovery provenance',
    status: 'Established philosophical problem + operationalization opportunity',
    prior: 'Computational discovery predates LLMs. Langley studied human–computer discovery cooperation; Clark & Khosrowi explicitly argue for a collective-centred account of AI-assisted discovery and discuss human recognition of significance. Priority scholarship separately studies disclosure, validation and credit.',
    position: 'The project no longer presents “does AI discover or facilitate discovery?” as a new question. The prospective contribution is operational: represent novelty, priority, contribution and epistemic authority separately across human–AI discovery episodes.',
    sources: [
      ['Langley 2000', 'https://doi.org/10.1006/ijhc.2000.0396'],
      ['Clark & Khosrowi 2022', 'https://doi.org/10.1007/s11229-022-03902-9'],
      ['Vale & Hyman 2016', 'https://doi.org/10.7554/eLife.16931'],
      ['AlphaDev 2023', 'https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-023-06004-9'],
    ],
  },
  {
    title: 'Human-Scale Epistemic Horizon',
    status: 'Underlying problem established · terminology candidate',
    prior: 'Computational opacity and computer-assisted mathematics already examine results that may be reliable without being surveyable or intuitively possessed by a human.',
    position: 'The useful question is the institutional and economic consequence of increasing opacity, not a claim that Agalmic Research discovered epistemic opacity.',
    sources: [
      ['Humphreys 2004', 'https://doi.org/10.1093/0195158709.001.0001'],
      ['AI and a priori mathematical knowledge', 'https://doi.org/10.1017/psa.2025.10160'],
    ],
  },
  {
    title: 'Memory Palace research graph',
    status: 'Standards integration / extension',
    prior: 'W3C PROV, RO-Crate, CRediT, MIRA and decision-provenance work already cover provenance, research-object packaging, contribution roles, claim/evidence graphs and contemporaneous decision records.',
    position: 'The Memory Palace is an application profile over those systems. Local extensions are reserved for authority state, search boundaries, branch state, reversal conditions and explicit epistemic handoff.',
    sources: [
      ['W3C PROV', 'https://www.w3.org/TR/prov-dm/'],
      ['RO-Crate 1.3', 'https://www.researchobject.org/ro-crate/specification/1.3/'],
      ['MIRA', 'https://www.mira.science/'],
    ],
  },
];

const reviews = [
  ['Discovery Attribution in Human–AI Science', 'Computational discovery, collective-centred discovery, priority, validation and machine novelty.', 'https://github.com/TsatsuAmable/agalmicresearch/blob/main/docs/LITERATURE_REVIEW_DISCOVERY_ATTRIBUTION.md'],
  ['Epistemic Handoff, Knowledge Brokering and Uptake', 'Absorptive capacity, transfer stickiness, brokers, intermediaries, transactive memory and expert-attention bottlenecks.', 'https://github.com/TsatsuAmable/agalmicresearch/blob/main/docs/LITERATURE_REVIEW_EPISTEMIC_HANDOFF.md'],
  ['Displaced Scarcity and Shifting Bottlenecks', 'Limiting factors, complements, attention, value capture, idea processing, induced innovation and current AI-abundance economics.', 'https://github.com/TsatsuAmable/agalmicresearch/blob/main/docs/LITERATURE_REVIEW_DISPLACED_SCARCITY.md'],
];
---
<Layout title="Intellectual Lineage" description="A living novelty and prior-art register for Agalmic Research, separating established antecedents from synthesis, extension, operationalization and unassessed novelty.">
  <header class="page-head">
    <div class="shell">
      <p class="eyebrow">Intellectual lineage & novelty · register v0.2</p>
      <h1>Do not reinvent the wheel and call the spokes a revolution.</h1>
      <p class="lede">Agalmic Research treats prior art as infrastructure. The aim is to discover what is already known, reuse it aggressively, and identify the narrower remainder that actually deserves investigation.</p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell thesis">
      <p class="eyebrow">Novelty discipline</p>
      <blockquote>Novelty is a conclusion of search, not a tone of voice.</blockquote>
      <p>An idea can be new to the curator, new to a model and even independently rediscovered without being new to the world. “I had not seen this before” is provenance. It is not prior-art clearance.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell split">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Correction</p>
        <h2>The project changes its claims when prior art changes the picture.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="large">Several ideas initially developed here have close or foundational antecedents.</p>
        <p>“Epistemic Economics” is now <strong>Economics of Epistemic Abundance</strong>. The “Law of Displaced Scarcity” is the <strong>Displaced Scarcity Hypothesis</strong>. Working Paper 001 has been revised to v0.3 to position the Authorial Capacity Constraint against prior work on metacognition, verification gaps, claim accountability, contributorship, epistemic dependence and computational opacity.</p>
        <p>The deeper reviews now narrow two further claims: generic epistemic routing is established in knowledge-brokerage and innovation-intermediary traditions, and the question of machine versus human discovery has a substantial computational and philosophical history.</p>
        <div class="callout"><p><strong>Agalmic abundance applies to ideas too: finding that someone already solved part of the problem is a gain, not a loss.</strong></p></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="section-number">Deep reviews</span>
        <div class="section-copy">
          <h2>Three branches have now had a second-pass literature review.</h2>
          <p>These notes record collisions, surviving deltas and search boundaries rather than hiding prior art behind a bibliography.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="index-list">
        {reviews.map(([title, summary, href], i) => (
          <article class="index-item">
            <span class="index-meta">R{String(i + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</span>
            <div>
              <h3><a href={href}>{title} ↗</a></h3>
              <p class="muted">{summary}</p>
            </div>
            <span class="index-status">deep review</span>
          </article>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="section-number">Living register</span>
        <div class="section-copy">
          <h2>What we currently think we are adding.</h2>
          <p>This is intentionally conservative and corrigible. New prior art should downgrade a claim rather than be squeezed into a footnote.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="index-list">
        {lineage.map((item, i) => (
          <article class="index-item">
            <span class="index-meta">{String(i + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</span>
            <div>
              <h3>{item.title}</h3>
              <p><strong>Antecedents:</strong> {item.prior}</p>
              <p><strong>Current position:</strong> {item.position}</p>
              <p class="muted">
                {item.sources.map(([label, href], j) => (
                  <><a href={href}>{label} ↗</a>{j < item.sources.length - 1 ? ' · ' : ''}</>
                ))}
              </p>
            </div>
            <span class="index-status">{item.status}</span>
          </article>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell split">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Distributed discovery</p>
        <h2>Generation, discovery credit and novelty are separate questions.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="large">The literature already rejects a simple one-actor picture of scientific discovery.</p>
        <p>Machine discovery research predates generative AI, and Clark & Khosrowi explicitly develop a collective-centred account of AI-assisted discovery. Our useful remaining task is operational rather than titular.</p>
        <div class="status-grid">
          <div><strong>Framing / direction</strong><span>Define the problem and constrain the search.</span></div>
          <div><strong>Generation</strong><span>Produce or surface a candidate result.</span></div>
          <div><strong>Recognition</strong><span>Select or notice that a candidate is worth pursuing.</span></div>
          <div><strong>Validation</strong><span>Supply proof, evidence, replication or expert scrutiny.</span></div>
          <div><strong>Integration</strong><span>Locate the result in the existing body of knowledge.</span></div>
          <div><strong>Realization</strong><span>Turn the warranted result into a usable object.</span></div>
        </div>
        <p>These are proposed provenance fields for study, not a claim to have discovered the constituent acts.</p>
        <div class="callout"><p><strong>Novelty is a relation between a result and prior knowledge. Discovery attribution is a relation between a discovery episode and its contributors.</strong></p></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell split">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Standards-first provenance</p>
        <h2>Reuse the machinery that already exists.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>Agalmic Research is migrating ordinary provenance to <a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/prov-dm/">W3C PROV</a>, research-object packaging to <a href="https://www.researchobject.org/ro-crate/specification/1.3/">RO-Crate</a>, and scholarly contribution roles to <a href="https://credit.niso.org/">CRediT</a>. The local graph is an extension layer rather than a rival standard.</p>
        <p><a href="https://github.com/TsatsuAmable/agalmicresearch/blob/main/docs/STANDARDS_PROVENANCE_PROFILE.md">Standards-first provenance profile ↗</a></p>
        <p><a href="https://github.com/TsatsuAmable/agalmicresearch/blob/main/docs/INTELLECTUAL_LINEAGE_AND_NOVELTY_REGISTER.md">Full novelty register v0.2 ↗</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell split">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Search boundary</p>
        <h2>This register does not certify novelty.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>The review is deepening but remains non-exhaustive. Scopus, Web of Science, EconLit, PhilPapers, ProQuest, patent databases, archival books and non-English literatures have not all been systematically exhausted. Technical Nemosyne/Moneta mechanisms remain outside scope.</p>
        <p class="muted">“Not found” means novelty remains unassessed, not that the project owns an intellectual island no one has visited.</p>
        <div class="actions">
          <a class="button" href={internal('provenance')}>Research provenance</a>
          <a class="button secondary" href={internal('publications/authorial-capacity-constraint')}>Working Paper 001</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>